{
  "id": "ports-iot-pipeline-ctmc",
  "title": "Sync vs async ports: IoT pipeline",
  "chapter": "Use case 5 — Synchronous vs asynchronous ports",
  "chapterOrder": 5,
  "formalism": "ctmc",
  "tool": "PRISM 4.x (or PRISM-games)",
  "summary": "Sensor → Gateway through a synchronous port (rendezvous), Gateway → Actuator through an asynchronous port (generated FIFO channel of capacity CAP_ch1, messages dropped when full).",
  "description": [
    "Synchronous connector Sensor.measure → Gateway.acquire: PRISM supports it directly — the two port actions are renamed to one shared label (sync_acquire_measure), so the sensor can only hand over a sample when the gateway is waiting.",
    "Asynchronous connector Gateway.forward → Actuator.apply: PRISM has no buffered channel, so the generator inserts a module ch1 (counter of messages in transit, capacity CAP_ch1). forward synchronises with the push, apply with the pop. Overflow policy DROP: a message sent to a full channel is lost and counted in the reward ch1_lost.",
    "Compare with the generated code (PRISM Code): the architecture is unchanged, only the connector semantics produces new modules, labels and rewards."
  ],
  "objectives": [
    "See which communication patterns PRISM supports natively and which need a transformation.",
    "Measure the effect of the buffer capacity on losses and throughput."
  ],
  "exercises": [
    "Vary CAP_ch1 from 1 to 10: how do R{\"ch1_lost\"} and R{\"ch1_received\"} evolve? How many states?",
    "Select the async connector and switch its overflow policy to BLOCK: what replaces the losses?",
    "Make the Sensor → Gateway connector asynchronous too (both ports ASYNC): what changes for the sensor?"
  ],
